# Crystal stage 2: Frenkel-Ladd switch between the real potential and an
# Einstein crystal, one spring constant per species.
#
# fix ti/spring mixes the forces itself, f = (1-lambda) f_pair + lambda (-k dx),
# so lambda = 0 is the full potential and lambda = 1 the Einstein crystal. One
# run covers the forward AND backward sweeps, which gives the hysteresis
# estimate for free.
#
# ORDER MATTERS: every ti/spring fix must come BEFORE the Langevin thermostat,
# or the thermostat force is scaled by (1-lambda) and the lambda = 1 endpoint
# is not the canonical Einstein crystal. That error costs ~185 meV/atom and is
# invisible except through the k-invariance test.
#
# Outputs: ti.dat (lambda, dU/dlambda; forward sweep then backward).

units metal
atom_style atomic
boundary p p p
read_data input.data

pair_style ${pair_style}
pair_coeff * * ${potential_path} ${species_string}

timestep ${time_step}
thermo ${thermo_interval}

${spring_groups}

velocity all create ${temperature} ${seed} mom yes rot yes
fix nv all nvt temp ${temperature} ${temperature} 0.1
run ${n_equil}
unfix nv

compute pe all pe/atom pair
compute pesum all reduce sum c_pe
fix nve all nve
${spring_fixes}
fix lan all langevin ${temperature} ${temperature} 0.1 39847 zero yes

variable lam equal f_ti1[1]
variable dU equal ((${spring_energy_sum})-c_pesum)/atoms
fix out all print 100 "${lam} ${dU}" file ti.dat screen no title "lambda dU"
run ${n_total}
